\texloomfrontmatter
\texloommainmatter

\chapter{Introduction}

Small farms waste a large share of their water because irrigation runs on fixed timers that ignore soil conditions and weather. This project designs a low-cost controller that waters only when the soil needs it.

\section{Problem Statement}

Commercial smart controllers cost more than most small farms can justify, and they depend on cloud services that rural areas cannot reach reliably.

\section{Objectives}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Measure soil moisture at three depths every 10 minutes.
\item Cut water use by at least 30\% compared with a timer schedule.
\item Keep the bill of materials under \$60 per zone.
\end{enumerate}

\chapter{Background}

Soil moisture sensing falls into two families: \emph{resistive} probes, which are cheap but corrode, and \emph{capacitive} probes, which last longer and are the choice for this design.

\section{Related Work}

Earlier student projects used Wi-Fi modules that drew too much power for solar operation. We use LoRa radios instead, trading bandwidth for range and battery life.

